    "summary": "The Galmudug State Electoral and Boundaries Commission announced official results for the House of Representatives, but opposition candidates and civil society groups have raised concerns about irregularities. The dispute highlights ongoing challenges to democratic consolidation in Somalia's federal states.",
    "content": "The Galmudug State Electoral and Boundaries Commission (GSEBC) declared the final results of the House of Representatives elections on Thursday, concluding a multi-phase voting process that began in late 2024. The announcement, made in the administrative capital Dhusamareb, confirmed the winners of the 89 seats contested across the state's districts.\n\nHowever, the outcome has been met with immediate objections from several losing candidates and local observers, who allege that vote counting in certain constituencies was not transparent and that the commission failed to address complaints in a timely manner. Protesters in two districts reportedly clashed with security forces, though no casualties have been confirmed.\n\nThe disputed election is a key step in Galmudug's broader political transition, following the 2021 indirect elections. The state parliament is responsible for electing a new president, and its composition will directly influence federal-level negotiations in Mogadishu. For the Somali diaspora, these results shape political alliances and resource distribution in a region with significant overseas investment.\n\nIndependent analysts note that while the process saw improved participation from minority clans compared with previous cycles, the lack of independent verification and the commission's limited dispute resolution capacity remain systemic weaknesses. The federal Ministry of Interior has called for calm, urging all parties to use legal channels.\n\nFor diaspora Somalis, particularly those from Galmudug, the stability of regional institutions affects remittance flows, business climate, and any potential return or investment plans. The crisis underscores the need for robust electoral management in federal states as Somalia continues its path toward universal suffrage.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/muran-ka-taagan-natiijada-doorashadii-barlamaanka-galmudug/424473/",
